WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.670
STEPHANIE WONG: We, as
developers, love serverless.

00:00:02.670 --> 00:00:05.100
We love that we can
focus on code, deploying,

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:07.420
and let the platform
take care of the rest.

00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:09.390
But we also keep
asking, what else

00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:11.110
can we deploy on serverless?

00:00:11.110 --> 00:00:13.590
What about going beyond
simple functions?

00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:15.540
Well, you can today
with Cloud Run,

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:18.705
which lets you run any stateless
container on serverless.

00:00:18.705 --> 00:00:21.510
With Cloud Run, you can
forget about infrastructure.

00:00:21.510 --> 00:00:24.930
It focuses on fast, automatic
scalings that's request-aware,

00:00:24.930 --> 00:00:26.850
so you can scale down
to zero and only pay

00:00:26.850 --> 00:00:28.260
when it's being used.

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:29.970
To demo this, I'm
going to deploy

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:32.220
a serverless microservice
that transforms

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:34.320
Word documents to PDFs.

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:36.300
To perform this
transformation, does

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:38.610
anyone remember OpenOffice?

00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:41.160
I'm going to simply add
OpenOffice inside my container,

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:43.500
and then run it in a
serverless environment.

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:47.100
Let's see how easy it is to run
it in a container on Cloud Run.

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:49.050
From the console,
go to Cloud Run

00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:51.900
and open the Deployment page.

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:59.240
Select or paste the URL
of the container image

00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:01.520
and click Create.

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:03.770
That's all we needed to
create a serverless container.

00:01:03.770 --> 00:01:07.340
No infrastructure to provision
in advance, no YAML file,

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:08.810
and no servers.

00:01:08.810 --> 00:01:12.350
Cloud Run has imported my image,
made sure that it started,

00:01:12.350 --> 00:01:15.590
and gathered a stable and
secure HTTPS endpoint.

00:01:15.590 --> 00:01:17.810
What we just deployed is
a scalable microservice

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:20.390
that transforms a
document into a PDF.

00:01:20.390 --> 00:01:22.308
Let's see it in action.

00:01:22.308 --> 00:01:23.600
Let's give it a doc to convert.

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:32.270
And we get a PDF back.

00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:35.220
OpenOffice is not exactly
a modern piece of software.

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:39.617
It's about a 15-year-old binary,
and it's about 200 megabytes.

00:01:39.617 --> 00:01:41.450
And we just took that
binary and deployed it

00:01:41.450 --> 00:01:43.130
as a serverless
workload with Cloud Run,

00:01:43.130 --> 00:01:46.100
because Cloud Run supports
Docker containers.

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:48.770
That means you can run any
programming language you want

00:01:48.770 --> 00:01:51.420
or any software in
a serverless way.

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:53.630
Let's look at the code.

00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:55.130
We have a small
piece of Python code

00:01:55.130 --> 00:01:58.550
that listens for
incoming HTTP requests

00:01:58.550 --> 00:02:01.901
and calls OpenOffice to
convert our document.

00:02:01.901 --> 00:02:04.730
And we also have a
very small Docker file.

00:02:04.730 --> 00:02:06.620
It starts by defining
our base image.

00:02:06.620 --> 00:02:09.620
In our case, it's the
official Python-based image.

00:02:09.620 --> 00:02:12.350
Later, we installed OpenOffice
and we specified our start

00:02:12.350 --> 00:02:13.650
command.

00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:15.680
Then, we packaged all
of this into a container

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:19.790
image using Cloud Build and
deployed it to Cloud Run.

00:02:19.790 --> 00:02:22.400
On Cloud Run, our
microservice can automatically

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:25.400
be scaled to thousands of
containers or instances

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:27.740
in just a few seconds.

00:02:27.740 --> 00:02:29.650
We just took a legacy
app and deployed it

00:02:29.650 --> 00:02:33.500
to a microservice environment
without any change in code.

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:36.290
But sometimes you might want to
have a little bit more control.

00:02:36.290 --> 00:02:40.650
For example, bigger CPU
sizes, access to GPUs,

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:43.640
more memory, or maybe have it
running on a Kubernetes Engine

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:44.840
cluster.

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:50.430
With Cloud Run on GKE, it
uses the exact same interface.

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:52.850
I'm going to deploy the
exact same container

00:02:52.850 --> 00:02:55.100
image, this time in GKE.

00:02:55.100 --> 00:02:57.650
And instead of a
fully-managed region,

00:02:57.650 --> 00:03:00.770
I'm now picking our GKE cluster.

00:03:00.770 --> 00:03:04.110
We get the same Cloud
Run developer experience.

00:03:04.110 --> 00:03:09.530
It's deploying, and our
microservice is creating.

00:03:09.530 --> 00:03:12.140
As before, we get a
stable and secure endpoint

00:03:12.140 --> 00:03:14.526
that automatically
scales our microservice.

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:21.120
Behind the scenes, Cloud
Run and Cloud Run on GKE

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:22.530
are powered by Knative--

00:03:22.530 --> 00:03:24.240
an opensource project
to run serverless

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:26.440
workloads that we
launched last year.

00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:28.020
This means we can
actually deploy

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:31.380
the exact same microservice to
any Kubernetes cluster running

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:32.400
on Knative.

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:34.260
Let's take a look.

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:37.890
I exported the microservice
into a file, service.yaml.

00:03:37.890 --> 00:03:39.720
Then, using the
kubectl command, I'll

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:44.500
deploy it to a managed Knative
on another cloud provider.

00:03:44.500 --> 00:03:50.675
I'll enter "kubectl get kservice
to retrieve the URL endpoint."

00:03:50.675 --> 00:03:51.610
And voila!

00:03:51.610 --> 00:03:54.303
We have it now on
another cloud provider.

00:03:54.303 --> 00:03:55.720
Let's look into
the service that's

00:03:55.720 --> 00:03:59.380
running by entering "gcloud
beta run services describe

00:03:59.380 --> 00:04:01.950
pdf service."

00:04:01.950 --> 00:04:03.650
If you're familiar
with Kubernetes,

00:04:03.650 --> 00:04:07.950
these API version and kind
fields may look familiar.

00:04:07.950 --> 00:04:10.470
In this case, we're
not using Kubernetes.

00:04:10.470 --> 00:04:13.830
But since Cloud Run implements
the Knative API, an extension

00:04:13.830 --> 00:04:16.230
of Kubernetes, this
is an API object

00:04:16.230 --> 00:04:18.430
that looks like Kubernetes.

00:04:18.430 --> 00:04:21.000
Knative enables services
to be run portably

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:23.820
between environments
without vendor lock-in.

00:04:23.820 --> 00:04:26.430
Cloud Run gives you everything
you love about serverless.

00:04:26.430 --> 00:04:29.430
There are no servers to manage,
you get to stay in the code,

00:04:29.430 --> 00:04:31.770
and there's fast scale-up,
and more importantly,

00:04:31.770 --> 00:04:33.390
scale down to zero.

00:04:33.390 --> 00:04:36.030
You get to pay nothing when
there are no cycles being run.

00:04:36.030 --> 00:04:38.040
Use any binary or
language, because it's

00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:39.905
on the flexibility
of containers.

00:04:39.905 --> 00:04:42.690
And it gives you access to
the Google Cloud ecosystem

00:04:42.690 --> 00:04:44.010
and APIs.

00:04:44.010 --> 00:04:45.780
And you get a
consistent experience

00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:46.940
wherever you want it--

00:04:46.940 --> 00:04:50.280
in a fully-managed
environment or on GKE.

00:04:50.280 --> 00:04:51.350
Thanks for watching.

00:04:51.350 --> 00:04:53.190
Check out more in the
description below.

00:04:53.190 --> 00:04:56.430
And subscribe to stay up-to-date
with the latest in serverless

00:04:56.430 --> 00:04:58.760
on Google Cloud.

00:04:58.760 --> 00:05:02.110
[MUSIC PLAYING]

